  <h2>What crops in the LinkedIn feed on mobile?</h2>

  <p>LinkedIn's pages give five rules for what survives in the feed. Two are written for mobile. The other three apply on every screen, phones included.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The 4:5 ceiling.</strong> A photo taller than 4:5 is centered and cropped. A 9:16 story frame loses its top and bottom, and text placed there goes with them.</li>
    <li><strong>The first picture sets the grid.</strong> In a multi-photo post, a landscape first image sits on top with the rest side by side below it; a portrait or square one takes the left column. When the rows run past 4:5, the bottom row is cropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Link images pad, they don't crop.</strong> On a Page post with a link, mobile shows a 1.91:1 image whole. Other shapes get thin white padding.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical ads are phone-only.</strong> LinkedIn's single image ad specs say paid vertical assets serve on mobile devices only, and square or vertical ad images may be cropped when the ad is shared organically.</li>
    <li><strong>Video edges belong to the interface.</strong> Keep text and logos off the top, bottom and sides, where LinkedIn's own controls can cover them.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The company cover is trimmed too, horizontally or vertically depending on the screen. LinkedIn says to keep key details in the center and away from the lower-right corner.</p>

