  <p class="source">Source: LinkedIn Marketing Solutions Help, "Video ads advertising specifications," read October 2026. https://www.linkedin.com/help/lms/answer/a424737</p>

  <p>Ad copy has two numbers per field: where it truncates and where it stops. Introductory text truncates after 150 characters on all three formats. Headlines truncate at 70 and stop at 200. Single image ads under 401 pixels wide show as a thumbnail.</p>

  <p>Document ads with a lead generation objective take PDFs only. Links inside a document work only after the viewer downloads it. LinkedIn's business spec page for document ads recommends staying under 10 pages.</p>

  <h2>Why LinkedIn uploads fail</h2>

  <p>LinkedIn's help pages name the common causes, and each one below comes from them.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>ProRes.</strong> It may upload, LinkedIn says, "but it will not be able to process." Video ads don't take it at all.</li>
    <li><strong>iCloud and a backgrounded app.</strong> The mobile app can't upload a video stored in iCloud, and an upload from iOS can stall if the app goes to the background.</li>
    <li><strong>Layered or mixed-size PDFs.</strong> Flatten the layers. Fit every page to one size. Animations and embedded video play as still images.</li>
    <li><strong>No title.</strong> LinkedIn's checklist for a failed document upload ends on it: make sure the document has a title.</li>
    <li><strong>Editing after posting.</strong> A posted photo can't be resized or edited, and a posted document can't be swapped. Delete the post and start again.</li>
    <li><strong>A link and a picture together.</strong> A post carries one or the other.</li>
    <li><strong>Video ads from a phone.</strong> Campaign Manager takes video uploads from a desktop only, 25 in 24 hours.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>LinkedIn gives document pages in inches, not pixels. Its document ad guidance lists standard layouts such as Letter, A4 and Legal, and asks for one page size throughout. Put the layout and that rule in the brief, whether the deck comes from your own team or from the <a href="/services/design/presentation-design">presentation design service</a>.</p>

  <h2>How hubStudio publishes to LinkedIn profiles and company pages</h2>

  <p>Connect LinkedIn once on My Connections. Your profile comes back, along with every company page LinkedIn lists you as an admin of. The connection lasts 60 days and renews with one click.</p>

  <p>In the LinkedIn module, pick a format (text only, one image, or a carousel of 2 to 8 slides), write a brief, and click Draft with AI, or Write it myself to type it with no AI run. The LinkedIn post format skill, picked by default, carries LinkedIn's posting rules. The editor counts down from 3,000 characters.</p>

  <p>Pictures come from a render, the Assets Library or an upload. The Image editor's LinkedIn panel offers Post, portrait at 1080 by 1350, plus square, landscape, Profile banner and Page cover, and checks size, file and wording before you save. Through hubStudio, LinkedIn takes JPG, PNG and GIF, not WebP, up to 10MB each. LinkedIn's own composer caps a photo at 5MB, so export under 5MB and both routes accept the file.</p>

  <p>Then publish now or schedule it, on up to 20 accounts per post. The queue is checked every five minutes, and a temporary error on LinkedIn's side is retried, three attempts in all. A post can go through Validation first, and it stays locked while it waits.</p>

  <p>Two shapes stay outside the app. Video doesn't go to LinkedIn from hubStudio yet, and a document post isn't one of its formats: publish those in LinkedIn's own composer. The <a href="/app/publish">publishing page of the app</a> covers the other networks.</p>

  <p>Publishing to LinkedIn costs nothing. Drafts and renders are charged to your prepaid balance, with the price shown before each run. Teams that would rather hand the whole channel over can read the <a href="/solutions/platforms/linkedin">LinkedIn platform page</a>, or brief the <a href="/services/design/social-media">social media design service</a>.</p>

